<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albigeois</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Albigeois is a historical region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a>, on the Tarn, corresponding largely to the modern Tarn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Its viscounts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trencavel">Trencavels</a>, held it under the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> were so numerous in the region that they were called Albigensians, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> launched against them in 1209 took its name from Albi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albigeois.jpg" alt="Albigeois" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Albi)_North_views_of_the_Ste_C%C3%A9cile_Cathedral_and_the_Old_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
